"""Run MAX's GPU tests on an Intel GPU through the DeviceContext compat
layer.

Run on the GPU host (it builds what the tests import, with
--//:hal_device_context=true):
    utils/check-max-gpu [-j JOBS] [-k SUBSTRING ...] [-o TABLE.md] [--logs DIR]
                        [--logs-all] [--expect tests/max-gpu-expected.txt]

Each test runs with `mojo run -D ASSERT=all --target-accelerator=intel:bmg-g31`
(assertions on, as Modular's test rules build them): the Intel compiler plugin
builds its kernels, and MAX's DeviceContext, built on the HAL (the compat
layer), runs them through the Level Zero driver plugin. A test whose source has
CHECK lines also has its stdout checked with FileCheck, as Modular's
mojo_filecheck_test does. A test is
  pass     exited 0 (or, for the one test that must crash, didn't exit 0), and
           its output passed FileCheck; or, for a test whose lit RUN line
           expects it not to compile, didn't compile, exited with an error
           code rather than by a signal, and its output passed FileCheck
           (stderr goes into stdout's pipe, as the RUN line's 2>&1 does, so
           the lines reach FileCheck in the order they were written);
  fail     compiled and ran, but failed (the first error is shown); or, for a
           test expected not to compile, compiled, or didn't compile but its
           output failed FileCheck, or died by a signal (as lit's `not`
           without `--crash` counts it), even after the expected errors and
           with FileCheck passing. Only the mojo process's own death by a
           signal counts as one: a crash in the program `mojo run` executes
           is caught by mojo, which prints "execution crashed" and exits 1,
           so it is judged as any other exit 1 is (lit's `not` inverts that
           exit 1 too);
  compile  didn't compile (the first error is shown);
  timeout  ran longer than the limit;
  n/a      didn't pass, and is a lit test whose RUN lines all build for
           another vendor's target.

Defines: a test's Bazel rule's `-D` copts are passed too (rules_mojo
passes `copts` to the compile). A lit test whose RUN commands that name no
target or an Intel one pass `-D` defines is run by those commands instead,
read as lit reads them (Modular's `%mojo...` substitutions, `not`, `2>&1`,
`| FileCheck %s --check-prefix=P`; a prefix with no lines fails, as in
FileCheck), each with -I and the plugins and, unlike lit, which adds no
target, our accelerator when it names none; it passes when they all pass. Its
commands that name only other vendors' targets aren't run.

utils/check-max-gpu --self-test checks how tests expected not to compile are
judged, and how RUN lines and Bazel copts are read, without Mojo or the GPU.

With --expect FILE (lines `<test> <result> <cause>`), each result is compared
with the file: `better` (now passes), `worse` (passed, now doesn't),
`changed` (another non-pass result) or `new`; the exit code is 1 when any test
got worse. Without it, 1 unless every test passed.

GPU faults: after a process whose kernel page-faults, the next process to
use the GPU hangs at its first wait. The kernel log's faults during the run
are matched to the tests running at the time; when there were any, each test
that timed out and was still running at or after the first fault is rerun
once, alone, after a throwaway GPU process, and reported `stranded` if the
rerun's result differs. Tests known to fault (FAULTING) run alone after the
others, followed by a throwaway process, so that they strand nothing.

Scope: the tests of max.gpu (max/mojo/test/gpu), MAX's GPU algorithm
and math tests, and the basic max/kernels GPU tests (basics, algorithm,
examples: elementwise, reductions, naive and tiled matmuls); MAX's
vendor-neutral matmul tests (LINALG_TESTS), the nn norm, RoPE and KV-cache
store tests (NN_NAMES, KVC_NAMES), the Gated DeltaNet's (SSM_NAMES), and
the paged flash-attention test per page and head size (VARIANTS).
"""

